WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.960
Welcome to another 
Stories From The Stores

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:10.360
I'm here with curator Katie Dabin and
today we've got a Dewar flask...

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:17.300
So this is a dewar flask for storing heads or brains in liquid nitrogen for cryogenic neuro-suspension.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:18.920
Is there a head in there?

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.920
Thankfully there isn't because that
would cause all sorts of headaches!

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.500
Okay but that sounds super sci-fi, super like Futurama.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:31.480
It sounds like a mad idea, but the reality is in medicine we use freezing all the time for cells and tissues

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.920
Think about IVF, we store embryos and sperm and cells, so it's real science.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:38.800
How does it actually work?

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.320
What you need to do is preserve the brain and the head,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.680
so you need to rapidly cool it to stop any cellular activity or any decay happening.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.680
You've got to take it down in three hours to stop any ice crystals forming

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.780
and then there's a longer two week process of taking it down to -196 celsius

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:00.600
and then these body parts are preserved for the future until they could be brought back to life.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.260
I'm pretty sure people like Walt Disney
and some famous people have had this, right?

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.520
Walt Disney was rumoured to be
frozen, but he never was he was actually buried

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.120
and in reality there's only been about 150 people who have been frozen by the

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.280
Alcor Life Extension Company - where
this object comes from - and 33 pets!

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:24.420
The first person to be frozen was Dr James Bedford and he was frozen in 1967,

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.820
he was an American professor of psychology at the University of California.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.360
Is this an unusual object for the museum to be collecting?

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.520
I think it's a really important object for the museum to collect

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:41.340
first of all it represents how a small group of scientists and members of society are responding to one of

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.800
mankind's biggest challenges - how to
overcome death

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.440
and really interesting ethical issues about how we think about the future

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:55.860
so are you you if your brain was put into a
different body, how long should somebody live for?

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.560
What rights would you have if you were living in the future if you were revived?

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:02.840
So you're a curator, would you want to be preserved like this?

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:07.940
I think even if medical science catches up and you could live with all your personality and memories,

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:14.340
I just think ultimately living in the future will be quite a lonely place, would your friends and family be there?

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.680
And I just think really you would be a dinosaur living outside your own time.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.700
You've got to understand that you haven't dissuaded me at all. I should live forever!

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:24.680
You should go for it.

